#!/bin/sh
#
# Every student-facing string that depends on the MACHINE THE STUDENT IS SITTING AT rather than
# on this container. Two questions live here, and they are keyed differently on purpose:
#
#   --copy / --link / --correct   the gesture to make, keyed on the TERMINAL (CS193V_TERM_CLASS),
#                                 with a host-OS branch inside the rows that need one.
#   --hostpath                    where the student's own copy of ~/projects is, and how they
#                                 reach it, keyed on the PLATFORM (CS193V_HOST_OS) alone.
#
# SO THERE IS NO TOP-LEVEL PLATFORM SWITCH, and adding one would misdescribe three of the four
# modes: the gesture rows are a per-terminal table that consults the OS, not a per-OS table. The
# dispatch is on the MODE first, and each mode then keys on whatever actually decides it.
#
# Installed as /usr/local/bin/cs193v-platform-messages. Callers: cs193v-shell sets @copy-hint and
# @select-correction from it at claim time, cs193v-linkbox prints its --link line, tmux.conf's
# shifted-drag bindings show the --correct line through the option cs193v-shell set, and the
# COURSE NOTES tell the agent to run --hostpath. That last one is the only mode a student's agent
# invokes directly; the rest are for the launcher. Running any of them by hand is harmless.
#
# ─── WHY THIS FILE EXISTS ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# tmux takes the mouse (`set -g mouse on`, tmux.conf Part 3) because it needs it for
# wheel-scrollback, tab clicks and the NEW TAB chip. A terminal in mouse-reporting mode has by
# definition given up its own click-drag selection: the modes say "stop interpreting gestures
# locally, send them as bytes". So selection has to come back some other way, and the only way
# any terminal offers is a BYPASS MODIFIER -- hold a designated key and the terminal keeps the
# gesture for itself.
#
# That modifier is in no standard. There is no capability, no terminfo entry and no query, so
# tmux cannot discover it. This file is the table tmux cannot have.
#
# Part 3 used to assert one answer for every terminal -- "SHIFT+drag is the route that works,
# everywhere, because it never reaches tmux" -- and that was measured false on the terminal this
# course is taught from. Terminal.app has no bypass at all: it ENCODES Shift into the mouse
# report (Cb=4) and forwards it, so the drag is swallowed by tmux and nothing is selected. Issue
# #123. The same capture eats CMD+double-click, so the login link cannot be opened either: #122.
#
# ─── WHY ONLY THESE FIVE TERMINALS ─────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Every terminal NOT named here bypasses with SHIFT -- kitty, Alacritty, WezTerm, Ghostty, foot,
# Warp, Konsole, xterm, each read from its own source or documentation -- and SHIFT is exactly
# what the default arm says. So the list is short because paring it COST NOTHING, not because the
# rest were overlooked. Specialisation is spent only where the generic answer is wrong:
#
#   Terminal.app     no bypass modifier at all. Shift is forwarded.
#   VS Code, macOS   no bypass either: macOptionClickForcesSelection defaults false, and
#                    xterm.js then returns `event.altKey && false` on a Mac while forwarding
#                    Shift. Same failure as Terminal.app, from the source rather than measured.
#   iTerm2           Option, hard-coded in PTYMouseHandler.m. SHIFT there EXTENDS an existing
#                    selection, so the generic advice is not merely useless but wrong.
#
# Windows Terminal and VTE each earn a row for their COPY KEY and their LINK CLICK, and neither
# of those is the bypass modifier -- which on both is SHIFT, exactly like the default arm's. That
# is the distinction to keep hold of when reading these two rows: `copy` says SHIFT and `link`
# says CTRL not through inconsistency but because they are two different mechanisms, a bypass
# modifier and an intercepted URL handler. Measured on Windows Terminal 1.24.11911 on 2026-09-05:
# SHIFT+drag under modes 1000/1006 printed nothing and highlighted text, and CTRL+click opened
# the browser. 50-image.sh asserts the pair together so a tidy-up cannot collapse them.
#
# THE DEFAULT ARM IS THE HISTORICAL WORDING, BYTE FOR BYTE, and 50-image.sh asserts that. It is
# what makes this change a no-op for every terminal the launcher cannot identify.
#
# ─── WHY THE TOKEN COMES FROM THE HOST ─────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Because nothing in here can work it out. tmux overwrites TERM_PROGRAM with "tmux" for every
# pane it spawns -- environ.c, unconditionally, since 3.2 -- so a pane cannot see the terminal
# that is really attached. The launcher's term_class() resolves it and forwards the token on the
# CLAIM exec, which is the exec that starts the tmux server and so the only one whose
# environment reaches a pane. See term_class in ./cs193v and 13-term-class.sh.
#
# A CLOSED SET OF TOKENS RATHER THAN THE RAW $TERM_PROGRAM, so no string a student controls ever
# reaches a tmux option or a display-message argument, and there is nothing here to escape.
#
# --hostpath IS THE ONE STUDENT-CONTROLLED STRING IN THIS FILE, and it does not weaken that: a
# course folder can be called anything, so CS193V_HOST_PROJECTS is arbitrary text -- but it is
# only ever printed to stdout for an agent to read, and never set as a tmux option, passed to
# display-message, or re-parsed by anything. The gesture modes are the ones tmux consumes, and
# they still carry nothing but tokens.
#
# ─── WHY `link` NAMES A CLICK ON SOME ROWS AND NOT THE REST ────────────────────
#
# THE RULE IS NOT "hardware or nothing", it is that a row may name a click only when the
# terminal's own URL handler is known to run BEFORE the mouse report is encoded. Hardware settles
# that; so does source that shows the handler ahead of the terminal's own reporting path. Nothing
# else does, and a click named on any other basis is a gesture that reaches tmux instead.
#
# Terminal.app, from hardware. Apple documents plain CMD+DOUBLE-click ("Hold down the Command key
# and double-click the URL" -- and CMD+single-click has never opened anything, which is what the
# old wording got wrong). Under reporting that is dead, and FN+CMD+double-click is what actually
# opened a link, confirmed by a local HTTP server receiving the request.
#
# vte, from hardware on ONE of the two terminals it covers. CTRL+click opened the link on Ptyxis
# 50.1. Ptyxis registers a CAPTURE-phase GtkGestureClick on the terminal widget
# (ptyxis-terminal.ui, click_capture) and its handler takes button 1 or 2 with GDK_CONTROL_MASK,
# resolves the URL through vte_terminal_check_hyperlink_at then check_match_at, and CLAIMS the
# gesture only if opening it succeeds -- otherwise it is set DENIED and the click goes on to tmux
# exactly as before. VTE's own click gesture is added with no phase set, and GTK defaults a
# controller to BUBBLE while running CAPTURE first, so Ptyxis is ahead of the report by
# construction rather than by luck. That is why the row names ONE modifier and not SHIFT+CTRL:
# there is no reporting left to bypass.
#
# GNOME TERMINAL IS THE HALF NOBODY HAS RUN, and the extension to it is knowing. Its
# terminal_screen_button_press overrides button_press_event, handles CTRL + button 1 or 2 on a
# hyperlink or a matched URL, and returns TRUE without ever chaining to VTE's parent handler --
# the same ordering, reached a different way. It shares Ptyxis's token, so it could not have been
# left on the old wording without splitting the token. tests/MANUAL.md 8.2 records which half is
# which.
#
# TILIX, XFCE4-TERMINAL AND TERMINATOR REACH THIS ROW TOO, through the bare-VTE_VERSION backstop
# in term_class -- VTE exports VTE_VERSION into every child it spawns, so every embedder lands
# here. Each implements its own CTRL+click rather than inheriting one from VTE, and none has been
# run. They are outside this course's target set and THE ROW IS DELIBERATELY NOT HEDGED FOR THEM:
# an "or hold SHIFT" clause would put the longer route first for everyone to protect terminals
# nobody in the course uses. Do not add one back.
#
# WINDOWS TERMINAL, FROM HARDWARE AND FROM SOURCE, AND THE SOURCE PREDICTED IT. Its ctrl+click
# hyperlink branch sits AHEAD of the VT-mouse test, with the reason in the code: "GH#9396: we
# prioritize hyper-link over VT mouse events". So the same ordering the two VTE embedders get
# structurally, Windows Terminal gets by an explicit decision -- which is the strongest form the
# rule above asks for. Confirmed on Windows Terminal 1.24.11911 (Windows 11 26200, 2026-09-05):
# CTRL+click opened the browser WITH MODES 1000 AND 1006 ACTIVE, which is the condition that
# settles it -- had the branch run after the report was encoded, the bytes would have gone to the
# pane and nothing would have opened. This row was gesture-free until that measurement and PR
# #146 is where the source argument was made.
#
# EVERYWHERE ELSE STAYS GESTURE-FREE, and there is reason to expect the composition to FAIL:
# xterm, foot and legacy conhost have no URL detection at all, and Alacritty's URL hint matches
# on NO modifier, so holding Shift may bypass reporting and still not open the link.
#
# VS CODE IS THE ROW THE SAME ARGUMENT WOULD FIT AND STILL DOES NOT GET, and the asymmetry is
# deliberate. xterm.js activates links from a listener the mouse-report path does not stop, which
# reads exactly like Windows Terminal's case -- but SOURCE ALONE HAS NEVER BEEN ENOUGH here for a
# terminal nobody has run, and GNOME Terminal is not the counter-example it looks like: it shares
# a token with a terminal that WAS run, so leaving it on the old wording would have meant
# splitting the token. VS Code shares with nothing. It keeps the instruction tests/MANUAL.md 8.2
# sanctions -- "39 characters can be retyped" -- until someone runs it. See PR #146.
#
# ─── SHELL ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# /bin/sh and a case statement, like open-url: no bashisms and nothing to source. The three
# gesture modes are called from a tmux command substitution at session-claim time, so they must
# be fast, must print exactly one line, and must never write to stderr.
#
# --hostpath IS THE ONE MODE THOSE LAST TWO DO NOT BIND, and the difference is worth stating so
# nobody "fixes" it: it is read by an AGENT rather than by tmux, so it prints the path on its own
# line and the instruction on a second, and nothing is laying it out in a box. Still no stderr,
# for the ordinary reason -- an agent that gets a diagnostic mixed into the answer relays it.

set -u

TOKEN="${CS193V_TERM_CLASS:-unknown}"
HOST_OS="${CS193V_HOST_OS:-}"
# ALREADY RENDERED FOR THE PLATFORM by gui_projects_path in cs193v-ui.sh, so this is a string to
# print and never one to translate: on WSL it is the \\wsl.localhost\... form File Explorer
# needs, and nothing in here would know how to build that. Empty on a container created before
# #257, or by a bare `podman run`, which --hostpath answers rather than ignoring.
HOST_PROJECTS="${CS193V_HOST_PROJECTS:-}"

# THE DEFAULT WORDING, defined once because three arms fall back to it and 50-image.sh pins the
# first one against a literal. Keep them in this order: what to hold, then what to press.
D_COPY='TO COPY: hold SHIFT and drag, then CTRL+SHIFT+C (CMD+C on a Mac).'
D_LINK='Select the link and paste it into your browser.'
D_CORRECT='SHIFT+drag reached the container instead of selecting -- see the guide.'

# SHIFT+drag that gets HERE means the terminal forwarded it rather than intercepting it, which is
# the definition of a terminal without a Shift bypass. So no `--correct` string may answer with
# "hold SHIFT" -- that is the advice that just failed. 50-image.sh asserts it for every token.

usage() {
    printf 'usage: cs193v-platform-messages --copy|--link|--correct|--hostpath\n' >&2
    exit 2
}

[ "$#" -eq 1 ] || usage

case "$1" in
--copy)
    case "$TOKEN" in
    apple-terminal)   printf 'TO COPY: hold FN and drag, then CMD+C.\n' ;;
    iterm2)           printf 'TO COPY: hold OPTION and drag, then CMD+C.\n' ;;
    vscode)
        # The only token that splits on the OS, which is why the host forwards the platform too.
        # On macOS there is no bypass until the student sets
        # terminal.integrated.macOptionClickForcesSelection; on Windows and Linux xterm.js falls
        # through to plain Shift. (VS Code's own docs say Alt there and are wrong.)
        case "$HOST_OS" in
        macos)        printf 'TO COPY: OPTION+drag -- VS Code needs one setting; see the guide.\n' ;;
        *)            printf 'TO COPY: hold SHIFT and drag, then CTRL+SHIFT+C.\n' ;;
        esac ;;
    # SHIFT is right on both of these; they are named for the copy key. CTRL+SHIFT+C and not
    # CTRL+C on Windows Terminal on purpose: its immutable defaults.json binds only
    # ctrl+shift+c, and ctrl+c comes from userDefaults.json written into the student's own
    # settings on first launch -- so anyone who upgraded or ever reset their settings does not
    # have it. CTRL+SHIFT+C is correct either way.
    #
    # VERIFIED ON A REAL INSTALL rather than from the docs, 2026-09-05, WT 1.24.11911: the
    # package's defaults.json binds `ctrl+shift+c`, `ctrl+insert` and `enter` to
    # Terminal.CopyToClipboard and does NOT bind ctrl+c -- while that machine's own
    # LocalState/settings.json DID carry a user-level ctrl+c, which is the userDefaults.json
    # mechanism above caught in the act. Both halves of the claim, from one machine.
    #
    # On Unix CTRL+C cannot be taken at all: it is the POSIX INTR character, and a terminal that
    # stole it would remove the only way to interrupt a runaway program.
    windows-terminal) printf 'TO COPY: hold SHIFT and drag, then CTRL+SHIFT+C.\n' ;;
    vte)              printf 'TO COPY: hold SHIFT and drag, then CTRL+SHIFT+C.\n' ;;
    *)                printf '%s\n' "$D_COPY" ;;
    esac ;;
--link)
    case "$TOKEN" in
    apple-terminal)   printf 'FN+COMMAND+double-click the link to open it.\n' ;;
    iterm2)           printf 'Hold OPTION to select the link, then paste it in your browser.\n' ;;
    vscode)
        case "$HOST_OS" in
        macos)        printf '%s\n' "$D_LINK" ;;
        *)            printf 'Hold SHIFT to select the link, then paste it in your browser.\n' ;;
        esac ;;
    windows-terminal) printf 'CTRL+click the link to open it.\n' ;;
    vte)              printf 'CTRL+click the link to open it.\n' ;;
    *)                printf '%s\n' "$D_LINK" ;;
    esac ;;
--correct)
    case "$TOKEN" in
    apple-terminal)   printf 'SHIFT does not select in Terminal.app -- hold FN and drag instead.\n' ;;
    iterm2)           printf 'Hold OPTION, not SHIFT, to select text here.\n' ;;
    vscode)
        case "$HOST_OS" in
        macos)        printf 'SHIFT does not select in VS Code -- see the course guide.\n' ;;
        *)            printf '%s\n' "$D_CORRECT" ;;
        esac ;;
    *)                printf '%s\n' "$D_CORRECT" ;;
    esac ;;
# ─── WHERE THE STUDENT'S OWN FILES ARE, AND HOW THEY OPEN THEM (#257) ─────────
# The one mode a student's AGENT runs rather than the launcher, and the only one keyed on the
# platform alone -- there is no terminal in the question.
#
# WHY THE AGENT NEEDS THIS AT ALL. Claude Code and codex only ever see /home/student/projects, so
# left to themselves they hand a student a path that does not exist on the student's computer.
# The notes tell them to run this and relay it.
#
# THE PATH FIRST, ON ITS OWN LINE, so it survives being quoted: it is the part a student pastes,
# and on Windows it is full of backslashes that must not be wrapped into prose.
#
# NO COMMAND IS EVER SUGGESTED TO A STUDENT HERE, and that is a course decision rather than a
# limitation: a student on a Mac or on Windows is not assumed to know a terminal, so they get a
# graphical route to a folder. `explorer.exe .` would be the shorter answer on WSL and is what
# Microsoft documents, but it is a command, and it cannot run in here anyway -- this container
# has no Windows interop.
#
# LINUX GETS THE PATH AND NOTHING ELSE, deliberately: that student is assumed to be at home in a
# terminal and a file manager, so a sentence naming one of the several Linux file managers would
# be wrong more often than it helped.
#
# AND AN UNKNOWN PLATFORM FALLS THROUGH TO THE SAME SILENCE as linux rather than guessing, while
# a MISSING PATH is answered instead of ignored. An empty answer is what teaches an agent to stop
# asking and reason from its own guesses, which is the failure this mode exists to prevent.
--hostpath)
    if [ -z "$HOST_PROJECTS" ]; then
        printf 'Not known in here -- this container was made without it.\n'
        printf 'Ask the student to run ./cs193v doctor on their own computer; it prints the folder.\n'
    else
        printf '%s\n' "$HOST_PROJECTS"
        # SOURCED HERE AND NOT AT THE TOP, so "nothing to source" stays true of the three gesture
        # modes -- they are called from a tmux command substitution at claim time and this arm is
        # not. The two sentences live in strings.sh because the LAUNCHER prints them too, and a
        # student who asks the agent and then runs doctor must not get two wordings of one
        # instruction; see that file.
        #
        # AND A MISSING FILE COSTS THE SENTENCE, NOT THE PATH. The path is already printed above,
        # which is the half that cannot be guessed, so an image whose /etc/cs193v is incomplete
        # still answers usefully rather than failing.
        # shellcheck source=cs193v-strings.sh
        [ -r /etc/cs193v/strings.sh ] && . /etc/cs193v/strings.sh
        case "$HOST_OS" in
        macos) [ -n "${CS193V_OPEN_MACOS:-}" ]   && printf '%s\n' "$CS193V_OPEN_MACOS" ;;
        wsl)   [ -n "${CS193V_OPEN_WINDOWS:-}" ] && printf '%s\n' "$CS193V_OPEN_WINDOWS" ;;
        esac
    fi ;;
*)  usage ;;
esac
